  {
   "item_id": 5083550,
   "item_text": "3",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post",
   "in_feed": "0",
   "is_watched": "0",
   "item_score": "0",
   "id": "8317143",
   "item_level": "4",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 5083550,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 1113185,
    "poster_id": 186972,
    "post_rendered": "Construct a tetromino by attaching two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e03b602154bf17643e7945df4dae9dcd3a22081.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" > dominoes along their longer sides such that the midpoint of the longer side of one domino is a corner of the other domino. This construction yields two kinds of tetrominoes with opposite orientations. Let us call them <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" >-</span> and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" >-</span>tetrominoes, respectively.<br>\nAssume that a lattice polygon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > can be tiled with <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" >-</span>tetrominoes. Prove that no matter how we tile <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > using only <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" >-</span> and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" >-</span>tetrominoes, we always use an even number of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" >-</span>tetrominoes.<br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Tamas Fleiner and Peter Pal Pach, Hungary</i>",
    "post_canonical": "Construct a tetromino by attaching two $2 \\times 1$ dominoes along their longer sides such that the midpoint of the longer side of one domino is a corner of the other domino. This construction yields two kinds of tetrominoes with opposite orientations. Let us call them $S$- and $Z$-tetrominoes, respectively.\nAssume that a lattice polygon $P$ can be tiled with $S$-tetrominoes. Prove that no matter how we tile $P$ using only $S$- and $Z$-tetrominoes, we always use an even number of $Z$-tetrominoes.\n\n[i]Proposed by Tamas Fleiner and Peter Pal Pach, Hungary[/i]",
